From: portare-ch <hello@portare.org>
Subject: [PATCH] ao_pipewire: block for the buffer lock in the process callback

The process callback took the AO buffer lock with a trylock and, when
the core held it, queued nothing: one cycle of silence, 512 samples at
44.1 kHz, and no message anywhere. The core takes that lock for a few
microseconds every playloop iteration, so on a three-core A510 cluster
playing music with a video filter graph the collision came about once a
minute, as an audible tick.

JACK, CoreAudio and SDL already block here. The lock is held for a
memcpy and a queue count, never across I/O, so the wait is bounded by
that and by a preempted core thread; a late cycle PipeWire can absorb,
a missing one it cannot.
---
--- a/audio/out/ao_pipewire.c
+++ b/audio/out/ao_pipewire.c
@@ -199,7 +199,7 @@ static void on_process(void *userdata)
     end_time += MP_TIME_S_TO_NS(time.buffered) / ao->samplerate;
     end_time -= pw_stream_get_nsec(p->stream) - time.now;
 
-    int samples = ao_read_data(ao, data, nframes, end_time, NULL, false, false);
+    int samples = ao_read_data(ao, data, nframes, end_time, NULL, false, true);
     b->size = samples;
 
     for (int i = 0; i < buf->n_datas; i++) {
